# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Never put real secrets in this file.
#
# The publishable key is safe in the browser: it grants nothing on its own,
# because every table is protected by row level security. The service role key
# is the opposite — it bypasses RLS entirely, so it belongs only in server-side
# secrets and must never appear in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable or in this repo.

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxx

# The canonical origin of this deployment. Every emailed link — confirmation,
# magic link, password reset — is built from this, never from a request header.
# No trailing slash. In production this is the real domain.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── The demo ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `npm run demo` sets this to 1 and starts the dev server on port 3200. You do
# not need to set it by hand, and you should not set it in a deployed
# environment: it replaces the database with an array in memory and the
# signed-in person with a cookie, and it does not enforce row level security.
#
# Everything else stays real. Every screen, every can() check, every menu
# derivation and every plan lock runs exactly as it does for a paying customer;
# only the rows and the identity are substituted. A screen that is broken is
# broken in the demo too, which is the point of it.
#
# The three variables above are still required even with the demo on, because
# `src/lib/env.ts` validates them when it is imported. Nothing connects to
# Supabase in demo mode, so the placeholder values in this file are enough.
#
# Left commented out on purpose. `@next/env` does not overwrite a variable that
# is already set, so an explicit `NEXT_PUBLIC_ESTIA_DEMO=0` copied into
# .env.local would be harmless — but a variable that only ever appears when the
# demo is wanted cannot be the reason the demo silently fails to start.
#
# Any value other than exactly `1` means "off".
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ESTIA_DEMO=1
#
# In a PRODUCTION build the line above is not enough on its own, and that is
# deliberate. Demo mode makes `proxy.ts` return early, which takes down the
# authentication wall for the whole application — right on a laptop, and the
# entire product served to anyone with the URL on a deployment. Because
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are baked in at build time, a stray `1` in a hosting
# provider's environment would be enough, with no runtime moment at which
# anybody notices.
#
# So a production build must be told twice, by a variable that says out loud
# what it permits. Without it the demo simply stays off and the real sign-in
# wall stands. Set it only for a showroom deployment that is meant to be open.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ESTIA_DEMO_ALLOW_PRODUCTION=1
